        "---\n",
        "title: \"Probability and Language\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Random tokens, conditional distributions, likelihood, entropy, and perplexity\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Natural language has uncertainty.  A sentence may continue in many possible ways, a word may have several meanings, and the same document may be assigned different labels depending on context.  This chapter develops the probabilistic viewpoint needed for modern NLP.  We treat tokens, sentences, labels, and model outputs as random objects.  The goal is to understand language modeling as conditional probability estimation and supervised NLP as learning probability distributions from data.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. describe a token sequence as a realization of random variables over a finite vocabulary;\n",
        "2. distinguish empirical token frequencies, population probabilities, and model probabilities;\n",
        "3. use the chain rule of probability to factor the probability of a sentence;\n",
        "4. explain why language modeling is conditional distribution estimation;\n",
        "5. derive maximum likelihood estimation for a unigram model;\n",
        "6. compute negative log-likelihood, cross-entropy, entropy, KL divergence, and perplexity;\n",
        "7. explain the difference between generative and discriminative NLP models;\n",
        "8. implement simple probability models for text using Python;\n",
        "9. use an interactive Plotly.js visualization to explore entropy, temperature, and next-token probabilities;\n",
        "10. use AI tools to critique probabilistic assumptions without treating the AI response as a proof.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why probability enters NLP\n",
        "\n",
        "In the previous chapters, we treated text as data: strings, token sequences, count vectors, and document-term matrices.  These representations are useful, but they do not yet answer questions such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- How likely is a sentence?\n",
        "- Which word is most likely to come next?\n",
        "- How uncertain is a model's prediction?\n",
        "- How should a classifier combine evidence from many words?\n",
        "- How should we compare two language models statistically?\n",
        "\n",
        "Probability provides the mathematical language for these questions.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the vocabulary is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_d\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A single token can be modeled as a random variable\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W:\\Omega \\to V,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\Omega$ is an underlying sample space.  For a sentence or document of length $T$, we use a sequence of random variables\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_1,W_2,\\ldots,W_T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with each $W_t$ taking values in $V$.  A particular observed sentence is a realization\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_{1:T}=(w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_T).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Central idea.**  A probabilistic language model assigns probabilities to token sequences:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_{1:T})=P(W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_T=w_T).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern large language models usually estimate conditional probabilities of the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_t \\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "A probability model does not claim that language is random in a philosophical sense.  Rather, it gives a mathematical way to represent uncertainty from the viewpoint of an observer who has partial information.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Probability distributions on a finite vocabulary\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed vocabulary $V=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_d\\}$, a probability distribution over tokens is a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p=(p_1,p_2,\\ldots,p_d) \\in R^d\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "such that\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_j \\geq 0, \\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j = 1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The set of all such vectors is the probability simplex\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Delta^{d-1}\n",
        "=\\left\\{p\\in R^d: p_j\\geq 0,\\ \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j=1\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If $p_j=P(W=v_j)$, then $p$ is the distribution of the random token $W$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{\\text{data},\\text{language},\\text{model},\\text{learns}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "One possible token distribution is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p=(0.35,0.25,0.30,0.10).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This means\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W=\\text{data})=0.35, \\qquad P(W=\\text{learns})=0.10.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A model's prediction at a particular position in a sentence is also a probability vector in a simplex.  If a neural language model reads the context\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language models\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "then it may output a conditional distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid \\text{language models}) \\in \\Delta^{d-1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The subscript $\\theta$ denotes trainable model parameters.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Empirical distributions\n",
        "\n",
        "In practice we do not know the true population distribution of language.  We observe a corpus and estimate probabilities from counts.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $c_j$ be the number of times token $v_j$ appears in a corpus and let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "N=\\sum_{j=1}^d c_j\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the total number of vocabulary tokens.  The empirical token distribution is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_j=\\frac{c_j}{N}, \\qquad j=1,\\ldots,d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a probability vector because $\\widehat p_j\\geq 0$ and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d \\widehat p_j\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d \\frac{c_j}{N}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{j=1}^d c_j\n",
        "=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Empirical distribution.**  A corpus turns counts into probabilities by normalization:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_j=\\frac{\\text{count of }v_j}{\\text{total token count}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the simplest statistical estimator in language modeling.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python example: empirical token probabilities"
      ],
      "id": "74b6ba7b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from collections import Counter\n",
        "import math\n",
        "\n",
        "corpus = [\n",
        "    \"language models learn from data\",\n",
        "    \"data models predict tokens\",\n",
        "    \"language data has structure\",\n",
        "    \"models learn probability\"\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "def tokenize(text):\n",
        "    return text.lower().split()\n",
        "\n",
        "tokens = []\n",
        "for doc in corpus:\n",
        "    tokens.extend(tokenize(doc))\n",
        "\n",
        "counts = Counter(tokens)\n",
        "N = sum(counts.values())\n",
        "empirical_p = {token: count / N for token, count in counts.items()}\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Token counts:\")\n",
        "print(counts)\n",
        "print(\"\\nEmpirical probabilities:\")\n",
        "for token, prob in sorted(empirical_p.items()):\n",
        "    print(f\"{token:12s} {prob:.3f}\")"
