      "source": [
        "The output is an empirical distribution on the vocabulary built from the corpus.  This distribution is not a deep model, but it is the mathematical beginning of language modeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Population distribution, data distribution, and model distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "It is useful to distinguish three distributions.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Distribution | Meaning | Usually known? |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| $p^*$ | true data-generating distribution of language in the target domain | no |\n",
        "| $\\widehat p$ | empirical distribution from the observed corpus | yes |\n",
        "| $p_\\theta$ | model distribution with parameters $\\theta$ | yes after training |\n",
        "\n",
        "The true distribution $p^*$ is an idealized object.  It represents the probability law of text in the population or domain we care about.  We never observe $p^*$ directly.  Instead, we observe samples and build an empirical approximation $\\widehat p$.  Training tries to choose parameters $\\theta$ so that $p_\\theta$ is close to $p^*$, using the corpus as evidence.\n",
        "\n",
        "A clean statistical formulation is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Choose }\\theta\\text{ so that }p_\\theta\\text{ assigns high probability to observed text.}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This principle leads to maximum likelihood estimation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Maximum likelihood for a unigram model\n",
        "\n",
        "The simplest language model assumes that tokens are independent and identically distributed from a vocabulary distribution $p$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let the corpus tokens be\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The unigram model says\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_N=w_N)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{i=1}^N p(w_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If token $v_j$ appears $c_j$ times, the likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(p)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{j=1}^d p_j^{c_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(p)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\log L(p)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d c_j\\log p_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We maximize $\\ell(p)$ subject to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_j\\geq 0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Ignoring zero-count boundary details, use a Lagrange multiplier:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal L(p,\\lambda)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d c_j\\log p_j\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\lambda\\left(1-\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For $c_j>0$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial \\mathcal L}{\\partial p_j}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{c_j}{p_j}-\\lambda=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_j=\\frac{c_j}{\\lambda}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using $\\sum_j p_j=1$ gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1=\\sum_{j=1}^d \\frac{c_j}{\\lambda}\n",
        "=\\frac{N}{\\lambda},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "so $\\lambda=N$.  Hence the maximum likelihood estimator is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_j=\\frac{c_j}{N}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**MLE for the unigram model.**  Under the independent-token model, the maximum likelihood estimate of the token probability is the empirical frequency:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_j=\\frac{c_j}{N}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This derivation is important because it shows how a simple counting rule is actually a likelihood-based statistical estimator.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Sentences and the chain rule\n",
        "\n",
        "A sentence is not just a bag of independent tokens.  Word order and context matter.  The full probability of a sequence is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_T=w_T).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The chain rule of probability gives the exact factorization\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_1,\\ldots,w_T)\n",
        "=\n",
        "P(w_1)P(w_2\\mid w_1)P(w_3\\mid w_1,w_2)\\cdots P(w_T\\mid w_1,\n",
        "\\ldots,w_{T-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^T P(w_t\\mid w_{1:t-1}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $w_{1:t-1}=(w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1})$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Language modeling problem.**  Estimate the conditional distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_t=\\cdot \\mid W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1}=w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A language model is a machine for producing this distribution.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula is exact.  The difficulty is statistical and computational: there are many possible histories $w_{1:t-1}$.  N-gram models solve this by truncating the history.  Transformers solve it by learning a flexible representation of the history through attention.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Conditional probability and prediction\n",
        "\n",
        "The basic object in next-token prediction is a conditional probability.  Given a context $C$, a language model outputs\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(v_j\\mid C),\\qquad j=1,\\ldots,d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a probability vector satisfying\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(v_j\\mid C)\\geq 0,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d p_\\theta(v_j\\mid C)=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The most likely next token is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat w\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\max_{v_j\\in V} p_\\theta(v_j\\mid C).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "But the full probability vector is more informative than only the maximum.  It tells us how uncertain the model is.  For example, after the context\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "machine learning models can\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "the model may assign high probability to several plausible next tokens such as \"predict,\" \"learn,\" \"classify,\" or \"generate.\"  A deterministic answer hides that uncertainty.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Negative log-likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a model assigns probability $p_\\theta(w_i)$ to each observed token $w_i$.  The likelihood of the observed corpus is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\theta)=\\prod_{i=1}^N p_\\theta(w_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^N \\log p_\\theta(w_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of maximizing log-likelihood, machine learning usually minimizes negative log-likelihood:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{NLL}(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\sum_{i=1}^N \\log p_\\theta(w_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence model, this becomes\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{NLL}(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\sum_{t=1}^T \\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_{1:t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The negative sign appears because optimization algorithms are often written as minimization algorithms.  Minimizing negative log-likelihood is equivalent to maximizing likelihood.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python example: negative log-likelihood"
      ],
