      "source": [
        "In NLP, entropy helps quantify uncertainty in token prediction.  A model with high next-token entropy is unsure among many possible continuations.  A model with low next-token entropy strongly prefers a small number of continuations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Cross-entropy\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose $p$ is the true distribution and $q$ is a model distribution.  The cross-entropy from $p$ to $q$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p,q)=-\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\log q_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This measures the expected negative log-probability assigned by model $q$ when the data are drawn from $p$.\n",
        "\n",
        "If $p$ is replaced by an empirical distribution $\\widehat p$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(\\widehat p,q)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\sum_{j=1}^d \\widehat p_j\\log q_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using $\\widehat p_j=c_j/N$, we get\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(\\widehat p,q)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\sum_{j=1}^d \\frac{c_j}{N}\\log q_j\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\frac1N \\sum_{j=1}^d c_j\\log q_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is average negative log-likelihood.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Cross-entropy and training.**  When we train a language model with next-token prediction, the usual loss is empirical cross-entropy:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\frac1N \\sum_{i=1}^N \\log p_\\theta(w_i\\mid \\text{context}_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus cross-entropy is not just an evaluation metric.  It is the main training objective for many language models.\n",
        "\n",
        "## KL divergence\n",
        "\n",
        "The Kullback-Leibler divergence from $p$ to $q$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^d p_j\\log\\frac{p_j}{q_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q)\\geq 0,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "with equality if $p=q$ on the support of $p$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The relationship between entropy, cross-entropy, and KL divergence is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p,q)=H(p)+D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\|q).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $H(p)$ does not depend on the model $q$, minimizing cross-entropy is equivalent to minimizing KL divergence from the data distribution to the model distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "This identity gives a clean mathematical interpretation of language-model training:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{training reduces the discrepancy between the data distribution and the model distribution.}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity is a common metric for language models.  If the average negative log-likelihood per token is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\overline L\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^N \\log p_\\theta(w_i\\mid \\text{context}_i),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}=\\exp(\\overline L).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If logarithms base $2$ are used, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}=2^{\\overline L_2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity can be interpreted as an effective number of equally likely choices.  For example, a perplexity of $10$ roughly means the model is as uncertain as if it were choosing among $10$ equally likely tokens at each step.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Important caution.**  Lower perplexity usually means better next-token prediction on similar data, but it does not automatically mean better reasoning, truthfulness, robustness, or usefulness.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interactive HTML: temperature, entropy, and next-token uncertainty\n",
        "\n",
        "The softmax function converts scores, or logits, into probabilities.  Given logits $z_1,\\ldots,z_d$ and temperature $T>0$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
