        "\n",
        "## Conditional language models as statistical estimators\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $C_t=(W_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1})$ denote the context before position $t$.  A conditional language model estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p^*(w_t\\mid C_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The model is a parameterized function\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_\\theta:C_t \\mapsto p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid C_t)\\in \\Delta^{d-1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Training data consist of context-target pairs\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(C_i,y_i),\\qquad i=1,\\ldots,N,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $y_i$ is the observed next token.  The empirical cross-entropy loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat R(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\frac1N\\sum_{i=1}^N \\log p_\\theta(y_i\\mid C_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the same mathematical structure whether $f_\\theta$ is an n-gram table, a recurrent neural network, or a transformer.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Unified view.**  Classical and neural language models differ in how they represent the context $C$ and parameterize $p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid C)$, but they share the same probabilistic target: estimate a conditional distribution over the next token.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## From counts to conditional probabilities\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple conditional model estimates probabilities from co-occurrence counts.  Let $a$ be a previous token and $b$ be a next token.  Define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c(a,b)=\\#\\{t: w_{t-1}=a,\\ w_t=b\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c(a)=\\sum_{b\\in V} c(a,b).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The empirical conditional probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat P(W_t=b\\mid W_{t-1}=a)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{c(a,b)}{c(a)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the bigram maximum likelihood estimator.  Chapter 5 develops this idea systematically.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python example: conditional next-token probabilities"
