        "\n",
        "## Zero probabilities and smoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "A serious problem occurs when a token sequence has not appeared in the training corpus.  If $c(a,b)=0$, then the maximum likelihood bigram estimate gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat P(b\\mid a)=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This means the model says the event is impossible.  But in language, unseen does not mean impossible.  A small training corpus cannot contain every valid phrase.\n",
        "\n",
        "A common remedy is smoothing.  For example, add-alpha smoothing estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat P_\\alpha(b\\mid a)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{c(a,b)+\\alpha}{c(a)+\\alpha d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\alpha>0$ and $d=|V|$.\n",
        "\n",
        "When $\\alpha=1$, this is called add-one smoothing or Laplace smoothing.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Smoothing principle.**  Smoothing moves probability mass away from observed events and assigns some mass to unseen events.  It is a statistical correction for finite data.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Chapter 5 studies smoothing in more detail for n-gram language models.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Generative and discriminative probabilistic models\n",
        "\n",
        "Many NLP tasks involve a label $Y$ and an input text $X$.  For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $X$ is a movie review and $Y$ is its sentiment label;\n",
        "- $X$ is an email and $Y$ indicates spam or not spam;\n",
        "- $X$ is a student question and $Y$ is the correct resource category.\n",
        "\n",
        "There are two common probabilistic viewpoints.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Generative modeling\n",
        "\n",
        "A generative model describes the joint distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X,Y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the product rule,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X,Y)=P(Y)P(X\\mid Y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For classification, Bayes' rule gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y\\mid X)=\\frac{P(X\\mid Y)P(Y)}{P(X)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Naive Bayes, developed in Chapter 6, is a generative text classifier.  It models how each class generates words.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Discriminative modeling\n",
        "\n",
        "A discriminative model directly estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y\\mid X).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression, neural classifiers, and transformer classifiers are usually discriminative models.  They do not need to model the probability of the text itself; they only model the label distribution given the text.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Type | Models | Main object |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Generative | Naive Bayes, language models | $P(X,Y)$ or $P(X)$ |\n",
        "| Discriminative | Logistic regression, neural classifiers | $P(Y\\mid X)$ |\n",
        "\n",
        "Both viewpoints are useful.  Generative models are often interpretable and useful with limited data.  Discriminative models are often more accurate when sufficient labeled data are available.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Bayesian updating for text classification\n",
        "\n",
        "Bayes' rule is central to probabilistic NLP classification.  Suppose $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$ is a class label and $X$ is a text.  Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid X)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{P(X\\mid Y=1)P(Y=1)}{P(X)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X)=P(X\\mid Y=0)P(Y=0)+P(X\\mid Y=1)P(Y=1),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "we can compute the posterior probability of the class once we have class priors and class-conditional text probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "This equation is the mathematical foundation of Naive Bayes classifiers.  Its practical success depends on how we model $P(X\\mid Y)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Worked example: a tiny probabilistic classifier\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose we classify short texts into two labels:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y\\in\\{\\text{positive},\\text{negative}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Assume equal class priors:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=\\text{positive})=P(Y=\\text{negative})=\\frac12.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let the text be represented by tokens.  A simple generative model estimates class-conditional token probabilities.  Suppose\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\text{good}\\mid \\text{positive})=0.30,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "P(\\text{good}\\mid \\text{negative})=0.05,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\text{clear}\\mid \\text{positive})=0.20,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "P(\\text{clear}\\mid \\text{negative})=0.10.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For the two-token document\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "good clear\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "and under the naive conditional independence approximation,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X\\mid Y=\\text{positive})\n",
        "=0.30\\cdot 0.20=0.060,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "while\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X\\mid Y=\\text{negative})\n",
        "=0.05\\cdot 0.10=0.005.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Multiplying by equal priors gives scores\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0.5\\cdot 0.060=0.030,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "0.5\\cdot 0.005=0.0025.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The posterior probability of the positive class is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{0.030}{0.030+0.0025}\\approx 0.923.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore the model predicts positive sentiment.\n",
