        "## Statistical assumptions in probabilistic NLP\n",
        "\n",
        "A mathematical NLP model is built from assumptions.  Some assumptions are explicit; others are hidden in the architecture or training data.\n",
        "\n",
        "Important assumptions include:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Sampling assumption.**  The training corpus is representative of the target distribution.\n",
        "2. **Tokenization assumption.**  The chosen tokens preserve the relevant information.\n",
        "3. **Context assumption.**  The model uses enough context to predict the target.\n",
        "4. **Stationarity assumption.**  The relationship between context and token does not change too much between training and deployment.\n",
        "5. **Loss assumption.**  Cross-entropy is an appropriate objective for the downstream goal.\n",
        "6. **Evaluation assumption.**  Test data represent the kinds of examples where the model will be used.\n",
        "\n",
        "These assumptions are not merely philosophical.  When they fail, a model may have low training loss but poor real-world behavior.\n",
        "\n",
