        "## Common mistakes\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mistake 1: Treating the most likely token as the whole model\n",
        "\n",
        "A language model returns a full probability distribution.  The argmax token is only one summary of that distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mistake 2: Confusing frequency with probability\n",
        "\n",
        "Empirical frequency estimates probability from data, but it is not the same as the unknown population probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mistake 3: Ignoring the role of the context\n",
        "\n",
        "The probability of a token depends on context.  For example, $P(\\text{bank})$ and $P(\\text{bank}\\mid \\text{river})$ represent different quantities.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mistake 4: Comparing raw likelihoods across corpora of different lengths\n",
        "\n",
        "Longer corpora usually have smaller raw likelihoods.  Use average log-likelihood, cross-entropy, or perplexity to compare across datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Mistake 5: Assuming low perplexity implies high-quality generation\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity measures next-token prediction on a distribution.  It does not directly measure truthfulness, reasoning, safety, or usefulness.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Chapter summary\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter introduced the probabilistic foundation of NLP.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. A token is modeled as a random variable taking values in a finite vocabulary.\n",
        "2. A sentence is a sequence of random variables.\n",
        "3. A language model assigns probabilities to token sequences or conditional probabilities to next tokens.\n",
        "4. Empirical frequencies estimate probability distributions from corpora.\n",
        "5. The unigram maximum likelihood estimator is the empirical token distribution.\n",
        "6. The chain rule factors a sentence probability into conditional next-token probabilities.\n",
        "7. Cross-entropy and negative log-likelihood are the central training losses for language models.\n",
        "8. Entropy measures uncertainty; perplexity exponentiates average negative log-likelihood.\n",
        "9. Generative models estimate $P(X,Y)$ or $P(X)$; discriminative models estimate $P(Y\\mid X)$.\n",
        "10. Probabilistic models are useful only when their assumptions match the intended data and task.\n",
